WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.176
Decide with a small eval on your task.

2
00:00:02.456 --> 00:00:04.333
Everything above narrows the field.

3
00:00:04.613 --> 00:00:07.600
The decision comes from testing candidates on your work.

4
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.461
A first eval does not need infrastructure.

5
00:00:10.741 --> 00:00:18.571
Collect 20 to 50 real inputs that represent your traffic, including the awkward ones (long, ambiguous, adversarial).

6
00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:25.549
Write down what a good output looks like as a short rubric: required facts, forbidden content, format, tone.

7
00:00:25.829 --> 00:00:41.488
Run the same inputs through two or three candidates, at least one of them a smaller tier, and record five things for each: a quality score from the rubric, cost per task from the usage fields, latency (look at the 95th percentile, not just the average,

8
00:00:41.768 --> 00:00:52.883
because slow outliers are what users remember), the provider’s data and retention terms, and operational fit (rate limits, regions, SDK support, how the model fails).

9
00:00:53.163 --> 00:00:55.573
Then decide like an engineer, not a fan.

10
00:00:55.853 --> 00:01:02.744
If two models pass your quality bar, the cheaper and faster one wins, and the margin funds the review time you will need.

11
00:01:03.024 --> 00:01:09.680
If none passes, the problem may be your prompt or missing context rather than the model, which the next weeks address.

12
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.571
Keep the eval set: the next model launch becomes an afternoon’s rerun instead of a debate.

13
00:01:15.851 --> 00:01:22.955
A provider’s reputation, a leaderboard rank, or a colleague’s favorite is a reasonable prior for what to test first.

14
00:01:23.235 --> 00:01:25.240
It is not evidence about your task.

15
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.960
Studio 3 turns this into a proper eval harness; the lab below is the first version.
